1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Everything in our world is under pressure.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
We just don't think about it much.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Pressure pushes water from a hydrant,

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
makes popcorn pop,

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
and cuts through steel like butter.

6
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
300 pounds of pressure, it's pretty loud.

7
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
It's an invisible force that we put to work,

8
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
trapping bubbles in a soft drink,

9
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:30,360
turning liquids into foamy cream,

10
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,680
and generating the most damaging force in an explosion.

11
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:40,740
Get ready to spend some time with modern marbles

12
00:00:40,741 --> 00:00:42,520
under pressure.

13
00:00:42,521 --> 00:00:56,521
Pressure is hard at work all around us.

14
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:02,020
An invisible force that makes things move faster,

15
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:05,820
change consistency, or raise in lower temperature.

16
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:10,100
Pressure is often hard to see,

17
00:01:10,101 --> 00:01:13,000
but it can be measured, whether it's in liquids,

18
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:15,200
in solids, or in gases.

19
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:20,140
Pressure is registered in pounds per square inch, or PSI.

20
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:22,040
That's literally the weight of one pound

21
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:25,140
pressing down on one square inch.

22
00:01:25,240 --> 00:01:29,060
Add weight, and the pressure goes up.

23
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:34,260
The water that comes from your tap, that's about 35 PSI.

24
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:40,100
The water shooting out of a fire hose, about 100 PSI.

25
00:01:40,101 --> 00:01:42,600
Engine 22 with a three-year-old.

26
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:51,620
Nobody understands the power of PSI like firefighters.

27
00:01:51,621 --> 00:01:55,080
Engine 22 of the Danbury, Connecticut Fire Department

28
00:01:55,081 --> 00:01:58,520
is a state-of-the-art mobile firefighting platform designed

29
00:01:58,521 --> 00:02:03,420
to do one thing, generate flame-killing water pressure.

30
00:02:03,421 --> 00:02:04,160
Lots of it.

31
00:02:04,161 --> 00:02:08,600
Engines like these are technological marvels,

32
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:12,600
capable of delivering more than 1,200 gallons per minute

33
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:15,700
via multiple hose connections.

34
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:18,100
That's because the engine's internal pump

35
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:20,640
creates enormous pressure that can push water out

36
00:02:20,740 --> 00:02:22,580
at top speeds.

37
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:26,680
Engine 22 only carries 500 gallons,

38
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:28,580
so these high-pressure hoses are going

39
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:31,520
to need more water fast.

40
00:02:31,521 --> 00:02:33,520
are going to need more water, fast.

41
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:40,120
In most fires, that water comes from a fire hydrant.

42
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:46,900
A five-inch diameter supply hose locks into the town's water

43
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,240
system.

44
00:02:49,340 --> 00:02:55,420
The pressure is on, and the hose springs to attention.

45
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:58,840
But municipal fire hydrants are unpredictable, delivering water

46
00:02:58,841 --> 00:03:01,840
at varying pressures.

47
00:03:01,841 --> 00:03:05,340
So the fire engine itself juggles the inflows and outflows,

48
00:03:05,341 --> 00:03:06,340
keeping the pressure constant.

49
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:12,080
Tackling a fire from a ladder truck

50
00:03:12,081 --> 00:03:14,760
adds another layer of complexity.

51
00:03:14,761 --> 00:03:17,360
The engine has to generate additional PSI

52
00:03:17,361 --> 00:03:21,120
to compensate for its height over the ground.

53
00:03:21,121 --> 00:03:22,440
22 driver, 24.

54
00:03:22,441 --> 00:03:23,160
Water is on the way.

55
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:28,580
It's like pumping 1,000 gallons of high-pressure water

56
00:03:28,581 --> 00:03:30,240
up a steep hill every minute.

57
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:33,960
You have big fire, you need big water.

58
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:37,720
Firefighters can't handle this volume of water by hand.

59
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:41,640
We could unsheathe all the shingles off this roof.

60
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:44,780
You're putting 1,000 gallons of water out

61
00:03:44,781 --> 00:03:46,720
at 80 pounds per square inch.

62
00:03:46,721 --> 00:03:47,700
That's a lot of force.

63
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:51,340
It's an awesome amount of power.

64
00:03:54,440 --> 00:03:57,140
Nothing is more critical to this high-pressure offense

65
00:03:57,141 --> 00:03:58,420
than a strong defense.

66
00:03:58,580 --> 00:04:02,780
And it comes from a fire hose, a flexible cylinder lined

67
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:04,980
with synthetic rubber.

68
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:07,080
This is a rubber extrusion machine.

69
00:04:07,180 --> 00:04:08,920
We're making a tube liner.

70
00:04:09,020 --> 00:04:11,620
So the rubber actually works really well in the fire hose

71
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:14,960
because it enables the fire hose to dilate and swell

72
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:16,740
and stretch under pressure.

73
00:04:16,840 --> 00:04:18,460
It also obviously provides sealant

74
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:20,540
for the water to transfer.

75
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:23,740
All-American Hose in Western Pennsylvania

76
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:28,580
uses about 2 and 1⁄2 tons of synthetic rubber every day.

77
00:04:28,581 --> 00:04:31,580
This liner will fit inside a sturdy outside covering

78
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:33,080
made of tightly woven nylon.

79
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:39,620
The weaving machine spins at 100 RPMs.

80
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:42,120
The weave is so tight, the machine's output

81
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:43,520
is only two feet per minute.

82
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:49,440
After weaving, each jacket is manually inspected

83
00:04:49,540 --> 00:04:52,440
for any defects that could cause the hose to weaken and burst

84
00:04:52,540 --> 00:04:53,840
under firefighting pressures.

85
00:04:53,841 --> 00:04:56,740
pressures.

86
00:04:56,840 --> 00:04:58,740
The jacket and rubber liner come together

87
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:02,640
on this 100-foot long table.

88
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:05,780
A fire hose is usually made of three sections.

89
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:08,680
One is the inner rubber liner, which we insert

90
00:05:08,780 --> 00:05:12,200
into the inner jacket.

91
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:14,800
And then we take that inner jacket liner assembly,

92
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:16,500
and we insert it into an outer jacket.

93
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:23,200
That protects the hose whenever it's under pressure.

94
00:05:23,201 --> 00:05:27,340
It also protects it when it's under a fire condition.

95
00:05:27,440 --> 00:05:30,740
Pressure seals the deal with an 80 PSI hot steam

96
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:31,940
treatment for 15 minutes.

97
00:05:35,440 --> 00:05:38,340
The steam expands the hose to operating size for the first

98
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:42,160
time and activates an adhesive that

99
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:45,660
binds the inner layers together.

100
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:48,400
The steam heat and pressure make the fire hose even more

101
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:49,200
rugged.

102
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:58,140
With the addition of alloy couplings, the fire hose is almost finished.

103
00:06:00,840 --> 00:06:06,560
But first, it must endure a proof test where it's subjected to 800 PSI.

104
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:10,900
That's two times greater than the maximum that firefighters will ever need.

105
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:16,840
Why? Because pressure can spike unexpectedly when hoses are twisted,

106
00:06:16,841 --> 00:06:22,760
crimped, or run over. Some hoses undergo an additional burst test over

107
00:06:22,761 --> 00:06:25,360
over 1,200 PSI.

108
00:06:25,361 --> 00:06:27,560
That's enough to break them open.

109
00:06:27,561 --> 00:06:32,640
But the way this hose is designed to burst along its seam, it could handle even that.

110
00:06:32,641 --> 00:06:37,320
It pumps up the hose to the burst pressure.

111
00:06:37,321 --> 00:06:42,000
And when the hose bursts, we want it to burst long-wise so it's safe like a banana.

112
00:06:42,001 --> 00:06:47,000
If it bursts in the cross-section, then it can be a loose cannon to get out the hose

113
00:06:47,001 --> 00:06:49,940
flying all around.

114
00:06:49,941 --> 00:06:54,040
It takes more than 1,200 psi to burst this hose,

115
00:06:54,041 --> 00:06:56,780
a key test of its ability to withstand enormous pressure.

116
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,620
But even to those of us not battling fires,

117
00:07:02,621 --> 00:07:06,580
water pressure plays an essential role.

118
00:07:06,581 --> 00:07:09,300
When you turn on the tap and water flows out,

119
00:07:09,301 --> 00:07:11,520
it's thanks to water pressure.

120
00:07:11,521 --> 00:07:13,620
But how does water pressure work?

121
00:07:13,621 --> 00:07:16,920
And why is it so reliable?

122
00:07:16,921 --> 00:07:20,420
As a liquid, all water naturally flows downhill,

123
00:07:20,421 --> 00:07:22,560
picking up power and pressure as it travels.

124
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:29,920
It's the same basic way water gets to your house.

125
00:07:29,921 --> 00:07:32,960
A water tower is simply a large water reservoir,

126
00:07:32,961 --> 00:07:35,620
elevated to generate pressure and propel water

127
00:07:35,621 --> 00:07:36,520
into your kitchen sink.

128
00:07:39,060 --> 00:07:41,920
Water towers use the most patient force I know,

129
00:07:41,921 --> 00:07:43,080
and that's gravity.

130
00:07:43,081 --> 00:07:46,440
All you have to do is rely on the mass of water

131
00:07:46,441 --> 00:07:47,720
to push it through the pipes,

132
00:07:47,721 --> 00:07:50,040
and for the most part, you're in pretty good shape.

133
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:55,260
Your typical water tower holds more than a million gallons,

134
00:07:55,261 --> 00:07:57,980
but its height is its most important feature.

135
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:03,440
For every two feet that water is raised off the ground,

136
00:08:03,441 --> 00:08:05,760
it contributes one more pound of pressure,

137
00:08:05,761 --> 00:08:07,440
or PSI, to the system.

138
00:08:08,680 --> 00:08:10,420
A water tower must be tall enough

139
00:08:10,421 --> 00:08:12,640
to push water through a network of pipes

140
00:08:12,641 --> 00:08:14,640
and into surrounding homes and businesses.

141
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:20,340
Most cities aim to generate pressure between 50 and 100 psi.

142
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:25,200
Water towers are usually taken out of service

143
00:08:25,201 --> 00:08:26,520
for maintenance once a year.

144
00:08:27,220 --> 00:08:29,920
This gives us a rare opportunity to step inside

145
00:08:29,921 --> 00:08:31,400
its pressure-creating construction.

146
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:38,540
This tank is over 90 feet in diameter,

147
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,860
and it can hold a million and a half gallons.

148
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:46,420
When the tank is full, there would be 35 feet of water

149
00:08:46,421 --> 00:08:48,760
above us.

150
00:08:48,860 --> 00:08:50,820
In Loudoun County, Virginia,

151
00:08:50,920 --> 00:08:53,520
Caldwell Tanks is now constructing what will be the

152
00:08:53,620 --> 00:08:56,660
country's biggest water tower site.

153
00:08:56,760 --> 00:08:59,960
These two massive 3 million gallon towers are half steel

154
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:03,400
plate and half reinforced concrete.

155
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:07,580
The pedestal has a diameter of 64 feet and 12 inch thick

156
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:09,340
walls all the way around.

157
00:09:09,440 --> 00:09:12,820
The pedestal height is on the order of 110 feet,

158
00:09:12,821 --> 00:09:17,820
including the dome that is on top of the pedestal.

159
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:20,560
When it's completed, the 500-ton tank

160
00:09:20,660 --> 00:09:22,960
will be hoisted up to the pedestal's dome

161
00:09:23,060 --> 00:09:24,960
and locked into position.

162
00:09:25,060 --> 00:09:29,480
This 116-foot diameter tank will deliver 50 pounds of pressure

163
00:09:29,580 --> 00:09:35,880
into the local water system, serving 15,000 households.

164
00:09:35,980 --> 00:09:37,620
But what happens to your water pressure

165
00:09:37,720 --> 00:09:39,420
if you live in a building that's taller

166
00:09:39,520 --> 00:09:41,620
than the highest water tower?

167
00:09:41,621 --> 00:09:45,160
That's a problem that New York City first faced more than 100

168
00:09:45,260 --> 00:09:48,120
years ago when it began to grow taller.

169
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:49,820
There wasn't nearly enough water pressure

170
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:52,360
for the topmost floors.

171
00:09:52,460 --> 00:09:56,360
The solution became an icon, a water tank for every building

172
00:09:56,460 --> 00:09:58,060
over 80 feet.

173
00:09:58,160 --> 00:10:02,580
Water tanks are old school, but they still work.

174
00:10:02,680 --> 00:10:05,580
Water tanks like these are replaced every 25 years.

175
00:10:05,581 --> 00:10:09,680
years.

176
00:10:09,681 --> 00:10:13,180
The job has to be completed in less than a day.

177
00:10:13,181 --> 00:10:17,380
A challenge American Pipe and Tank is ready to take on.

178
00:10:17,381 --> 00:10:19,880
While this tank is empty, this building has no fire

179
00:10:19,881 --> 00:10:20,920
protection.

180
00:10:20,921 --> 00:10:23,340
So it's pretty critical that gets back online as soon as

181
00:10:23,341 --> 00:10:24,041
possible.

182
00:10:32,440 --> 00:10:35,200
The team spends the first two hours dismantling the old

183
00:10:35,201 --> 00:10:36,440
water tank.

184
00:10:36,540 --> 00:10:37,340
Watch it.

185
00:10:43,100 --> 00:10:45,440
Next, they assemble a barrel large enough

186
00:10:45,540 --> 00:10:48,940
to supply this nine-story building.

187
00:10:49,040 --> 00:10:50,540
5,000 gallons should do it.

188
00:10:53,460 --> 00:10:58,260
The team erects a circle of staves, and then

189
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:00,560
tightens them into position by a series of loops.

190
00:11:00,561 --> 00:11:07,560
All of this while dangling more than 100 feet over the pavement.

191
00:11:07,561 --> 00:11:11,560
In New York City, all water tanks, old and new,

192
00:11:11,561 --> 00:11:12,560
are made of wood.

193
00:11:12,561 --> 00:11:16,560
Wood is a great material for an outdoor water tank.

194
00:11:16,561 --> 00:11:18,560
Wood is a natural product.

195
00:11:18,561 --> 00:11:19,560
It breathes.

196
00:11:19,561 --> 00:11:21,560
It does very well outdoors.

197
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:27,560
When you actually look at this tank from the top,

198
00:11:27,561 --> 00:11:30,560
you will look down and you will see a true surface.

199
00:11:30,561 --> 00:11:34,560
perfectly milled and installed like a big jigsaw puzzle.

200
00:11:34,561 --> 00:11:37,060
Like all water towers, once completed,

201
00:11:37,061 --> 00:11:39,060
the tank will require very little attention

202
00:11:39,061 --> 00:11:41,060
to do its pressure-generating job.

203
00:11:41,061 --> 00:11:45,060
The water tank is a very maintenance-free type system.

204
00:11:45,061 --> 00:11:47,560
A small pump in the basement fills the tank,

205
00:11:47,561 --> 00:11:49,560
and gravity just does the rest.

206
00:11:51,560 --> 00:11:54,560
While New Yorkers are proud of their rooftop water tanks,

207
00:11:54,561 --> 00:11:58,560
some landmarks prefer to keep them out of sight.

208
00:11:58,561 --> 00:12:01,560
The Empire State Building was one of the first skyscrapers

209
00:12:01,561 --> 00:12:03,560
to hide its water up top.

210
00:12:05,560 --> 00:12:08,560
So even if you don't see them, water towers and rooftop tanks

211
00:12:08,561 --> 00:12:12,560
are up there, providing constant water and steady pressure.

212
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:23,560
Countless aspects of our everyday lives are under pressure.

213
00:12:23,561 --> 00:12:26,560
We harness water pressure and store it high in the air

214
00:12:26,561 --> 00:12:29,660
in the air until we need it.

215
00:12:29,760 --> 00:12:34,340
We bottle up air pressure and hold it in aerosol cans.

216
00:12:34,440 --> 00:12:37,600
It may be everywhere, but pressure is not always

217
00:12:37,700 --> 00:12:38,480
under our control.

218
00:12:42,780 --> 00:12:45,320
Explosions.

219
00:12:45,420 --> 00:12:46,920
This is pressure gone wild.

220
00:12:50,860 --> 00:12:54,320
An explosion causes rapid expansion,

221
00:12:54,321 --> 00:12:57,220
which gives birth to an unpredictable pressure wave

222
00:12:57,320 --> 00:12:59,020
that can travel over long distances.

223
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:08,160
But what's really going on here?

224
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:11,140
Imagine a pebble dropping into a pond.

225
00:13:11,240 --> 00:13:13,980
The ripples move out at a steady pace.

226
00:13:14,080 --> 00:13:18,080
An explosion is something like this, but in the open air,

227
00:13:18,180 --> 00:13:20,380
and not at all steady.

228
00:13:20,480 --> 00:13:23,140
Shock waves from an explosion can only move as fast

229
00:13:23,141 --> 00:13:25,980
as the speed of sound, so they bunch up,

230
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:28,780
and that's what creates a wallop-packing pressure wave.

231
00:13:32,380 --> 00:13:35,880
But pressurized gases aren't always destructive.

232
00:13:35,980 --> 00:13:40,120
On a much smaller scale, we even bottle air's explosive pressure

233
00:13:40,220 --> 00:13:43,620
and enjoy it, bubble by bubble.

234
00:13:43,720 --> 00:13:46,660
Avery's Beverages has been putting the pop in soda pop

235
00:13:46,760 --> 00:13:48,440
for more than a century.

236
00:13:48,540 --> 00:13:51,340
If you want to see what 300 pounds of pressure is like,

237
00:13:51,440 --> 00:13:52,400
I can open a valve here.

238
00:13:52,401 --> 00:13:54,400
It's pretty loud, though, I'll warn you ahead of time.

239
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:02,400
Rob Metz is the mastermind

240
00:14:02,401 --> 00:14:05,400
who keeps these decades-old machines running.

241
00:14:05,401 --> 00:14:07,400
We're an old-fashioned soda company,

242
00:14:07,401 --> 00:14:10,400
and it's, as you can see, handmade.

243
00:14:10,401 --> 00:14:14,400
And we still make our soda the way it was done years ago.

244
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:21,400
That means first mixing up a batch of flavored syrup,

245
00:14:21,401 --> 00:14:25,860
and then adding the other half of the recipe, the fizz.

246
00:14:26,800 --> 00:14:30,360
The bubbles in a soft drink are carbon dioxide, or CO2.

247
00:14:30,640 --> 00:14:33,240
That's why we call the process of putting the bubbles in

248
00:14:33,241 --> 00:14:34,440
carbonation.

249
00:14:35,840 --> 00:14:37,900
Pressure plays a role in every step.

250
00:14:39,160 --> 00:14:41,520
Avery stores their CO2 in a liquid state,

251
00:14:41,980 --> 00:14:45,640
under 300 pounds of pressure, at 40 degrees below zero.

252
00:14:46,720 --> 00:14:48,780
This is where we control the amount of pressure

253
00:14:48,781 --> 00:14:50,240
going into the carbonator,

254
00:14:50,241 --> 00:14:52,620
and this is where we adjust it for the different flavors.

255
00:14:52,720 --> 00:14:56,400
For fruity flavor, we're going to have it running around 60 pounds per square inch.

256
00:14:56,560 --> 00:15:00,420
For a higher carbonated mixer, a cola, a ginger ale,

257
00:15:00,421 --> 00:15:03,140
we're going to run it at about 80 pounds per square inch.

258
00:15:04,280 --> 00:15:06,020
Higher pressure means more bubbles.

259
00:15:06,780 --> 00:15:07,760
And you heard right.

260
00:15:08,760 --> 00:15:11,240
Colas contain more bubbles than fruit-flavored sodas.

261
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:16,600
That's simply because we like our colas much fizzier than other carbonated beverages.

262
00:15:16,601 --> 00:15:20,600
So how does CO2 find its way into water?

263
00:15:20,601 --> 00:15:23,600
Coming in the top here is our water line,

264
00:15:23,601 --> 00:15:27,600
and in the side here is where our carbon dioxide comes.

265
00:15:27,601 --> 00:15:31,600
Normally, you can't see the moment when the fizz goes in.

266
00:15:31,601 --> 00:15:35,600
Here, inside the carbonator, pressurized carbon dioxide

267
00:15:35,601 --> 00:15:38,600
is forced into the water, and a unique reaction occurs

268
00:15:38,601 --> 00:15:40,600
between the water and the gas.

269
00:15:40,601 --> 00:15:45,600
Carbon dioxide is special because it dissolves in the water.

270
00:15:45,601 --> 00:15:50,040
You can get a lot more CO2 into a bottle like this one

271
00:15:50,140 --> 00:15:52,800
than you can nitrogen or oxygen,

272
00:15:52,900 --> 00:15:55,640
and that's because the CO2 is more soluble.

273
00:15:55,740 --> 00:15:58,840
By the time it's done, this water will have absorbed

274
00:15:58,940 --> 00:16:01,720
three times its own volume in carbon dioxide.

275
00:16:01,820 --> 00:16:04,060
That is a lot of gas.

276
00:16:04,160 --> 00:16:06,980
And the higher the pressure, the faster the gas dissolves

277
00:16:07,080 --> 00:16:08,060
into the water.

278
00:16:10,160 --> 00:16:14,400
The soda finally comes together at the bottling station.

279
00:16:14,401 --> 00:16:19,500
where the flavored syrup gets dispensed and mixed with the carbonated water under pressure.

280
00:16:24,180 --> 00:16:27,920
Then a capper seals it off and traps the CO2.

281
00:16:30,260 --> 00:16:33,780
When they get down here, the syrup and the carbonated water aren't mixed.

282
00:16:34,200 --> 00:16:36,740
So every bottle is going to be inverted three times.

283
00:16:36,860 --> 00:16:39,320
One, two, three.

284
00:16:39,720 --> 00:16:41,160
And then they're mixed and stay mixed.

285
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:42,500
So that's what's happening over here.

286
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:48,140
Now it's time to sit back and enjoy.

287
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:49,640
Just don't shake things up.

288
00:16:52,540 --> 00:16:54,940
Soda pop is by no means the only beverage

289
00:16:55,040 --> 00:16:57,740
in which we find CO2 bubbles.

290
00:16:57,840 --> 00:17:00,240
A celebratory champagne pop?

291
00:17:00,340 --> 00:17:04,060
That's the result of CO2 escaping the bottle.

292
00:17:04,160 --> 00:17:07,260
Shake the champagne, and you excite the gas,

293
00:17:07,360 --> 00:17:10,300
creating a surge of bubbles and pressure.

294
00:17:10,400 --> 00:17:14,060
Even agitated, much of the CO2 remains.

295
00:17:14,061 --> 00:17:17,400
there's still plenty of fizz to be enjoyed.

296
00:17:17,500 --> 00:17:20,700
But what if nearly every single CO2 bubble in a bottle

297
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:23,500
is forced out all at once?

298
00:17:28,300 --> 00:17:31,480
That's what you're seeing here in the infamous Coke Mentos

299
00:17:31,580 --> 00:17:34,740
explosion.

300
00:17:34,840 --> 00:17:37,720
A roll of candy is dropped into a bottle of cola,

301
00:17:37,820 --> 00:17:41,880
and the pressure release is downright explosive.

302
00:17:41,980 --> 00:17:44,060
How does it work?

303
00:17:44,061 --> 00:17:46,900
Most scientists believe that the unusual surface

304
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,300
of the candy spurs a rapid chain reaction

305
00:17:49,400 --> 00:17:52,560
to bubble formation, in which each new bubble creates

306
00:17:52,660 --> 00:17:58,300
even more bubbles and an instant release of all the pressure

307
00:17:58,400 --> 00:18:02,220
until the bottle is out of gas.

308
00:18:02,320 --> 00:18:05,420
So the explosive frog is all the carbon dioxide

309
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:06,720
leaving the bottle at once.

310
00:18:09,420 --> 00:18:12,160
Like an explosion, there's lots of pressure,

311
00:18:12,161 --> 00:18:15,500
but it's released at a much slower rate.

312
00:18:15,600 --> 00:18:19,500
No pressure wave, no damage.

313
00:18:19,600 --> 00:18:22,000
Just one big mess.

314
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:28,500
Pressure kicks off many of our days.

315
00:18:28,600 --> 00:18:31,420
It's the driving force behind another foamy mess.

316
00:18:34,120 --> 00:18:37,120
Only when this one blows up, you put it on your face.

317
00:18:42,160 --> 00:18:46,160
When Barbasol was invented in 1919,

318
00:18:46,260 --> 00:18:48,560
it was the world's first shaving cream.

319
00:18:48,660 --> 00:18:51,000
But it didn't become the fluffy stuff we know today

320
00:18:51,100 --> 00:18:56,500
until the 1950s with the advent of the pressurized aerosol can.

321
00:18:56,600 --> 00:19:00,580
Before shaving cream, men had to lather up with soap.

322
00:19:00,680 --> 00:19:02,980
Shaving methods have evolved since then,

323
00:19:03,080 --> 00:19:06,580
but the materials, not so much.

324
00:19:06,680 --> 00:19:08,960
A visit to a Barbasol plant in Ohio

325
00:19:09,060 --> 00:19:11,360
reveals that shaving creams still share a key

326
00:19:11,361 --> 00:19:16,009
share a key ingredient with soap, stearic acid.

327
00:19:16,010 --> 00:19:20,460
It starts as flakes, but it slowly transforms

328
00:19:20,461 --> 00:19:23,852
into a liquid as it heats to 150 degrees.

329
00:19:23,853 --> 00:19:27,740
Emulsifiers, sudsing agents, and filtered water

330
00:19:27,741 --> 00:19:34,340
are combined with stearic acid in massive 3,000-gallon mixing tanks. The pressure is

331
00:19:34,341 --> 00:19:35,340
is yet to come.

332
00:19:38,060 --> 00:19:41,340
Over 10,000 empty cans enter the production line every hour.

333
00:19:44,940 --> 00:19:48,340
A 12-head filler processes 180 of them a minute.

334
00:19:51,180 --> 00:19:54,060
Well, the shaving cream at this stage pretty much looks like,

335
00:19:54,061 --> 00:19:56,300
I would compare it to skim milk.

336
00:19:56,301 --> 00:19:58,540
Fairly watery and cloudy.

337
00:19:58,541 --> 00:20:00,460
It's not until the propellant goes in the can

338
00:20:00,461 --> 00:20:02,300
that it gets the foamy, rich texture.

339
00:20:04,340 --> 00:20:09,340
Once valves are crimped to the top of each can,

340
00:20:09,440 --> 00:20:12,240
they're ready to receive pressure.

341
00:20:12,340 --> 00:20:15,180
But for that step, the entire production line

342
00:20:15,280 --> 00:20:17,680
makes a detour out of the factory building.

343
00:20:19,620 --> 00:20:22,420
The pressure is injected in a gas house that's

344
00:20:22,520 --> 00:20:24,300
separate from the factory.

345
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:28,300
Barbasol uses two gases as propellants, propane

346
00:20:28,400 --> 00:20:30,200
and isobutane.

347
00:20:30,300 --> 00:20:33,060
Both are potentially explosive, which

348
00:20:33,061 --> 00:20:37,060
which is why this process is done outside the factory walls.

349
00:20:37,061 --> 00:20:39,060
So if there were a spark present,

350
00:20:39,061 --> 00:20:42,060
the roof would actually get blown off the building

351
00:20:42,061 --> 00:20:47,060
and this room would get foamed to suppress the fire.

352
00:20:47,061 --> 00:20:49,060
The amount of propellant in a single can

353
00:20:49,061 --> 00:20:52,060
is not enough for a consumer to worry about.

354
00:20:52,061 --> 00:20:58,060
Once the cans leave this building, they are under pressure.

355
00:20:58,061 --> 00:21:02,060
The cans return to the factory under 80 PSI.

356
00:21:02,061 --> 00:21:05,660
Then they run through a hot bath to ensure that there are no leaks.

357
00:21:06,960 --> 00:21:09,800
The heat increases pressure to over 100 psi,

358
00:21:10,960 --> 00:21:13,740
simulating conditions the cans might encounter during shipping.

359
00:21:15,900 --> 00:21:20,640
But how does shaving cream go from the consistency of skim milk to this?

360
00:21:22,580 --> 00:21:25,760
You have to understand what's going on inside the can.

361
00:21:26,460 --> 00:21:29,020
The propellant gases mix with the soapy solution.

362
00:21:29,021 --> 00:21:33,020
Under pressure, they become a single liquid.

363
00:21:33,120 --> 00:21:36,020
When the valve opens, the pressure pushes the liquid out,

364
00:21:36,120 --> 00:21:37,260
and foamy cream emerges.

365
00:21:41,160 --> 00:21:44,760
The material, the propellant, is above its boiling point

366
00:21:44,860 --> 00:21:45,900
at room temperature.

367
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:49,380
As a result, when I open the valve,

368
00:21:49,480 --> 00:21:52,680
I release the pressure, and the propellant boils.

369
00:21:52,780 --> 00:21:55,780
That small amount of liquid turns into a huge amount of gas,

370
00:21:55,781 --> 00:21:59,980
And that's the way you're able to get a large amount of foam

371
00:22:00,080 --> 00:22:02,980
into a small can of shaving cream.

372
00:22:03,080 --> 00:22:05,420
So inside a can of shaving cream,

373
00:22:05,520 --> 00:22:08,120
pressure reveals what a versatile force it can be.

374
00:22:15,740 --> 00:22:18,040
Once you start looking for it, pressure

375
00:22:18,140 --> 00:22:23,540
is just about everywhere, from the mundane to the critical.

376
00:22:23,541 --> 00:22:26,040
It plays a role in nearly every job.

377
00:22:28,540 --> 00:22:31,540
Cut something, and it's pressure that does the work.

378
00:22:33,540 --> 00:22:37,540
It doesn't matter what's in your hand or what you're dividing.

379
00:22:39,540 --> 00:22:43,540
The best cutter in the world employs blistering pressure,

380
00:22:43,541 --> 00:22:47,540
yet it's cutting with nothing more than water.

381
00:22:47,541 --> 00:22:50,540
It's a great demonstration of the incredible power

382
00:22:50,541 --> 00:22:52,540
of liquids under pressure.

383
00:22:52,541 --> 00:22:55,840
But where does all that force come from?

384
00:22:55,940 --> 00:22:59,740
JetEdge Waterjet Systems has built a machine that puts water

385
00:22:59,840 --> 00:23:01,140
under massive pressure.

386
00:23:01,240 --> 00:23:04,680
It's called, appropriately enough, a water intensifier

387
00:23:04,780 --> 00:23:06,280
system.

388
00:23:06,380 --> 00:23:09,160
A hydraulic pump turns at 100 horsepower,

389
00:23:09,260 --> 00:23:12,200
generating 3,000 psi.

390
00:23:12,300 --> 00:23:15,760
This pressure is transferred to a cylinder that uses a piston

391
00:23:15,860 --> 00:23:20,160
to put 75,000 pounds of pressure on water.

392
00:23:20,161 --> 00:23:24,600
If 75,000 pounds of pressure per square inch sounds like a lot,

393
00:23:24,700 --> 00:23:26,060
it is.

394
00:23:26,160 --> 00:23:27,800
Consider this.

395
00:23:27,900 --> 00:23:31,400
Nuclear subs can't survive deeper than 2,400 feet

396
00:23:31,500 --> 00:23:34,000
in the ocean, where the pressure is a little more

397
00:23:34,100 --> 00:23:35,500
than 1,000 psi.

398
00:23:42,920 --> 00:23:48,560
Water exits the nozzle at Mach 2, roughly 2,000 miles an hour.

399
00:23:48,561 --> 00:23:51,560
That's faster than a bullet exits a gun.

400
00:23:51,561 --> 00:23:55,560
A jet of pressurized water has a lot in common

401
00:23:55,561 --> 00:23:57,560
with another high-tech tool.

402
00:23:57,561 --> 00:24:00,560
The ability to concentrate this stream of water

403
00:24:00,561 --> 00:24:03,560
into a very small area,

404
00:24:03,561 --> 00:24:06,560
it's kind of like using water as a laser,

405
00:24:06,561 --> 00:24:10,560
because that's what a laser beam also does as well.

406
00:24:10,561 --> 00:24:13,560
The precision of a water cutter is unparalleled.

407
00:24:13,561 --> 00:24:16,560
The water jet blasts through a sheet of carbon fiber

408
00:24:16,561 --> 00:24:19,600
of carbon fiber as if it were butter.

409
00:24:19,601 --> 00:24:24,360
And it'll do the same to 12-inch steel or titanium.

410
00:24:24,361 --> 00:24:26,700
First of all, the water acts as a lubricant.

411
00:24:26,701 --> 00:24:29,300
And second, it also acts as a cooling agent

412
00:24:29,301 --> 00:24:31,340
to take some of that heat away.

413
00:24:31,341 --> 00:24:35,780
Unlike a water jet, other cutting devices generate heat,

414
00:24:35,781 --> 00:24:37,920
which can warp the material or make it brittle.

415
00:24:41,880 --> 00:24:44,520
A water jet is unstoppable.

416
00:24:44,521 --> 00:24:47,720
In fact, if it weren't for a two-foot deep bath of water

417
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:50,020
underneath the material being cut,

418
00:24:50,120 --> 00:24:53,260
the water jet would be carving holes in the factory floor.

419
00:24:53,360 --> 00:24:54,960
Water can cut a number of things.

420
00:24:55,060 --> 00:24:58,160
You can cut sheet metal, foam, rubber.

421
00:24:58,260 --> 00:25:00,640
But if you want to cut something really thick,

422
00:25:00,740 --> 00:25:02,800
like, say, six inches of titanium,

423
00:25:02,900 --> 00:25:05,200
then you've got to add garnet abrasive.

424
00:25:05,300 --> 00:25:10,120
And that is this stuff right here.

425
00:25:10,220 --> 00:25:12,020
The abrasive adds erosive power

426
00:25:12,021 --> 00:25:14,660
to the pressurized water and accelerates the cutting.

427
00:25:17,660 --> 00:25:19,860
Water jet cutting has been a boon to industries

428
00:25:19,960 --> 00:25:22,360
like auto racing.

429
00:25:22,460 --> 00:25:24,860
Michael Waltrip Racing fields multiple teams

430
00:25:24,960 --> 00:25:27,560
on the NASCAR circuit.

431
00:25:27,660 --> 00:25:30,340
They build dozens of cars in their North Carolina facility

432
00:25:30,440 --> 00:25:31,140
every year.

433
00:25:34,340 --> 00:25:36,720
Custom parts cut by high pressure water

434
00:25:36,820 --> 00:25:39,240
are used every step of the way.

435
00:25:39,340 --> 00:25:41,320
Removing the engine reveals all the parts cut

436
00:25:41,321 --> 00:25:42,520
cut by the water jet.

437
00:25:43,660 --> 00:25:45,700
Here we have a bare chassis.

438
00:25:45,701 --> 00:25:47,860
We can see a large number of components

439
00:25:47,861 --> 00:25:49,560
that we cut from the water jet,

440
00:25:49,561 --> 00:25:54,340
from our engine mounts here and on the back of the engine.

441
00:25:54,341 --> 00:25:57,700
Suspension pickup parts are all cut from the water jet.

442
00:25:57,701 --> 00:26:00,240
We've got steering arm braces where the steering connects

443
00:26:00,241 --> 00:26:01,740
is all cut on the water jet.

444
00:26:03,140 --> 00:26:05,740
The water jet cutter shapes the parts quickly

445
00:26:05,741 --> 00:26:08,240
and to an engineer's precise specifications.

446
00:26:08,241 --> 00:26:15,740
When you're traveling at over 200 miles per hour, where failure can be a matter of life

447
00:26:15,741 --> 00:26:21,320
and death, you want to know that every part works perfectly.

448
00:26:21,321 --> 00:26:28,480
By machining parts flawlessly, a water jet cutter makes racing safer.

449
00:26:28,481 --> 00:26:34,520
Pressurized water is ideal for cutting, and pressurized air can be just as useful.

450
00:26:34,521 --> 00:26:38,760
Although it lacks the weight of water, air pressure can turn into something that'll blow

451
00:26:38,761 --> 00:26:40,900
you away.

452
00:26:40,901 --> 00:26:48,080
With a sandblast, these machines clean the toughest surfaces, all powered by compressed

453
00:26:48,081 --> 00:26:50,040
air.

454
00:26:50,041 --> 00:26:55,920
Nothing beats a sandblaster at scouring paint or grit and grime from old parts.

455
00:26:55,921 --> 00:27:00,420
And just like with a water jet, the secret ingredient is another gritty abrasive that's

456
00:27:00,421 --> 00:27:02,080
driven by pressure.

457
00:27:02,081 --> 00:27:04,280
With pressurized media, you don't have to go anything over that.

458
00:27:04,281 --> 00:27:07,880
I normally recommend 90 to 100 for blasting.

459
00:27:07,881 --> 00:27:10,120
When it gets fully decompressed, that handle will drop,

460
00:27:10,121 --> 00:27:12,480
and that tells you you're ready to fill.

461
00:27:12,481 --> 00:27:14,420
Now you're ready to fill.

462
00:27:14,421 --> 00:27:17,460
If a 100 PSI sandblaster sounds lame

463
00:27:17,461 --> 00:27:22,100
compared to the pressures of a 75,000 PSI water jet cutter,

464
00:27:22,101 --> 00:27:23,300
it's not.

465
00:27:23,301 --> 00:27:29,940
At 100 PSI, a sandblaster will strip naked skin to the bone.

466
00:27:29,941 --> 00:27:34,140
Bad Boy Blasters, manufacturer's custom sandblasters,

467
00:27:34,141 --> 00:27:38,080
and every single one relies on pressurized air.

468
00:27:38,081 --> 00:27:41,380
But blasting with abrasives is a dirty, dusty job

469
00:27:41,381 --> 00:27:44,420
and can require a lot of cleanup.

470
00:27:44,421 --> 00:27:47,020
They've invented a vacuum that sucks away the abrasives

471
00:27:47,021 --> 00:27:50,720
and paint as fast as it can be blasting.

472
00:27:50,721 --> 00:27:54,520
The paint dust stays in the air filter.

473
00:27:54,521 --> 00:27:55,221
Turn on the vacuum.

474
00:27:55,222 --> 00:28:04,560
You see at the 120 PSI, this is running a 40-60 grit.

475
00:28:04,561 --> 00:28:09,000
How nice of a, that's a good taintable service.

476
00:28:09,001 --> 00:28:12,600
A self-contained blast cabinet made of welded sheet metal

477
00:28:12,601 --> 00:28:16,400
makes sandblasting even less messy.

478
00:28:16,401 --> 00:28:20,340
Sandblasters are highly efficient, yet despite the name,

479
00:28:20,341 --> 00:28:23,180
they don't use sand.

480
00:28:23,181 --> 00:28:26,940
Sand can cause silicosis, a potentially fatal lung disease.

481
00:28:28,060 --> 00:28:31,720
Most industrial sandblasters like these now use aluminum oxide,

482
00:28:32,420 --> 00:28:34,900
the same material commonly found on sandpaper.

483
00:28:36,440 --> 00:28:39,380
Aluminum oxide can survive multiple runs through a blaster.

484
00:28:40,860 --> 00:28:41,720
I can blast anything.

485
00:28:41,840 --> 00:28:45,920
I got people doing anything from blasting glass, doing designs, metal parts,

486
00:28:45,921 --> 00:28:47,980
a lot of aircraft industry work.

487
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:51,380
It may not pack the punch of a water jet,

488
00:28:51,381 --> 00:28:53,880
But a blast of air pressure is all it takes

489
00:28:53,980 --> 00:28:57,780
to turn an old hunk of metal into something shiny and new.

490
00:29:04,920 --> 00:29:06,720
Pressure is everywhere.

491
00:29:06,820 --> 00:29:09,640
Just look up.

492
00:29:09,740 --> 00:29:14,540
The atmosphere rises more than 20 miles over our heads.

493
00:29:14,640 --> 00:29:19,540
And all that air has weight, a very precise weight, in fact,

494
00:29:19,541 --> 00:29:22,380
14.7 pounds.

495
00:29:22,480 --> 00:29:24,380
That's how much is pressing down on those of us

496
00:29:24,480 --> 00:29:29,880
who live at sea level all day, and we hardly notice.

497
00:29:29,980 --> 00:29:32,280
But there's something that does get our attention,

498
00:29:32,380 --> 00:29:35,480
atmospheric pressure, lows, and highs.

499
00:29:35,580 --> 00:29:37,400
We know it as weather.

500
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:42,000
Air pressure has a lot to do with weather,

501
00:29:42,100 --> 00:29:45,000
and particularly it has a lot to do with wind.

502
00:29:45,100 --> 00:29:46,900
Check out any weather map.

503
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:48,700
The highs and lows are air systems seeking

504
00:29:48,701 --> 00:29:51,000
seeking to equalize pressure.

505
00:29:51,100 --> 00:29:53,100
Wind blows from areas of high pressure

506
00:29:53,200 --> 00:29:55,800
into those with low pressure.

507
00:29:55,900 --> 00:29:57,180
If you want to think of it that way,

508
00:29:57,280 --> 00:29:59,440
the more steep is the drop off.

509
00:29:59,540 --> 00:30:00,940
From high pressure to low pressure,

510
00:30:01,040 --> 00:30:03,940
the more severe is the weather.

511
00:30:04,040 --> 00:30:07,280
Up there, it's a maelstrom of varying pressures, temperatures

512
00:30:07,380 --> 00:30:08,960
and humidities.

513
00:30:09,060 --> 00:30:12,960
And in extreme cases, it turns deadly.

514
00:30:13,060 --> 00:30:13,960
Look at that.

515
00:30:13,961 --> 00:30:18,960
But air doesn't exert nearly as much pressure

516
00:30:19,060 --> 00:30:23,500
as the other substance that rings our planet, water.

517
00:30:23,600 --> 00:30:27,000
30 feet of seawater creates the same amount of pressure

518
00:30:27,100 --> 00:30:29,400
as 20 miles of air.

519
00:30:29,500 --> 00:30:32,920
Some of the most destructive forces humans must overcome

520
00:30:33,020 --> 00:30:35,920
are in the deep sea.

521
00:30:36,020 --> 00:30:40,320
At just 100 feet, water pressure crushes an empty water bottle.

522
00:30:40,420 --> 00:30:43,960
That's how much the air inside it has compressed.

523
00:30:43,961 --> 00:30:46,960
Of course, you wouldn't be seeing images like these at all

524
00:30:46,961 --> 00:30:49,960
if it weren't for divers with video cameras.

525
00:30:49,961 --> 00:30:51,960
We're under a lot of pressure,

526
00:30:51,961 --> 00:30:55,960
time pressure, water pressure, to do a good job.

527
00:30:55,961 --> 00:30:59,960
Highly trained underwater cameramen like Bob Cranston

528
00:30:59,961 --> 00:31:02,960
put their bodies on the line every time they dive.

529
00:31:02,961 --> 00:31:04,960
And the video gear they take down with them

530
00:31:04,961 --> 00:31:07,960
is worth tens of thousands of dollars.

531
00:31:07,961 --> 00:31:11,960
For divers, crushing pressures are the enemy.

532
00:31:11,961 --> 00:31:17,520
And what we're feeling when we go in the water is the weight of the water over our hands.

533
00:31:18,200 --> 00:31:20,820
The deeper we go, the more pressure there is.

534
00:31:23,460 --> 00:31:25,020
Waterproofing alone won't do it.

535
00:31:25,680 --> 00:31:31,720
The only way you can take a camera underwater is by using a pressure-resistant shell called an underwater housing.

536
00:31:32,640 --> 00:31:35,980
Gates Underwater Products designs housings for all types of cameras.

537
00:31:36,920 --> 00:31:39,280
The process involves a series of trade-offs.

538
00:31:39,281 --> 00:31:42,280
The shell needs to be big enough to fit the camera,

539
00:31:42,281 --> 00:31:45,280
yet cylindrical in shape to resist pressure.

540
00:31:45,281 --> 00:31:49,280
The fewer the parts, the fewer things that can go wrong.

541
00:31:51,280 --> 00:31:54,280
The main section of the housing emerges from a 65-and-a-half-pound

542
00:31:54,281 --> 00:31:57,280
block of solid aluminum.

543
00:31:59,280 --> 00:32:04,280
Machines remove 88% of the metal, leaving a hollow 8-pound

544
00:32:04,281 --> 00:32:06,280
cylinder.

545
00:32:09,280 --> 00:32:13,280
Then it's on to the tumbler, which polishes away any sharp

546
00:32:13,380 --> 00:32:15,180
edges with synthetic rocks and water.

547
00:32:18,120 --> 00:32:20,620
But even if all parts are perfectly machined,

548
00:32:20,720 --> 00:32:24,620
a seal can always fail due to human error.

549
00:32:24,720 --> 00:32:26,940
The user has to do a visual inspection

550
00:32:27,040 --> 00:32:29,640
to make sure there is nothing on the O-ring that

551
00:32:29,740 --> 00:32:31,040
can compromise it.

552
00:32:31,140 --> 00:32:33,840
Something as small as, say, a human hair,

553
00:32:33,940 --> 00:32:37,000
laying across the O-ring can allow water to pass at that

554
00:32:37,100 --> 00:32:39,280
point.

555
00:32:39,281 --> 00:32:44,280
Large silicone o-rings sealed big openings against pressure.

556
00:32:44,281 --> 00:32:48,280
And tiny sleeves, called glands, employ a double set of seals

557
00:32:48,281 --> 00:32:52,280
to maintain pressure on the knobs and shafts that control the camera.

558
00:32:52,281 --> 00:32:55,280
As the last step before plunging into the crushing ocean,

559
00:32:55,281 --> 00:32:58,280
workers test the housing's ability to survive.

560
00:33:01,280 --> 00:33:06,280
First, they test for normal pressure by sucking all the air out of the housing.

561
00:33:09,280 --> 00:33:12,780
Now we wait to see if there's any changes in the numbers

562
00:33:12,781 --> 00:33:14,620
on the pressure gauge.

563
00:33:14,621 --> 00:33:16,780
If normal air pressure can't find its way

564
00:33:16,781 --> 00:33:18,280
into the vacuum in the housing,

565
00:33:18,281 --> 00:33:21,320
then it's ready for a much more serious pressure test.

566
00:33:21,321 --> 00:33:23,320
The housing didn't lose any pressure.

567
00:33:23,321 --> 00:33:25,320
It's perfectly sealed.

568
00:33:31,400 --> 00:33:34,240
But how will it do when the pressure is multiplied?

569
00:33:34,241 --> 00:33:36,280
So now I'm adding pressure to the tank,

570
00:33:36,281 --> 00:33:39,280
which will bring us up to at least 100 degrees.

571
00:33:39,281 --> 00:33:44,280
and simulate our dive to about 225 feet.

572
00:33:50,320 --> 00:33:53,120
Thorough leak testing requires putting every moving part

573
00:33:53,121 --> 00:33:53,821
to the test.

574
00:33:56,220 --> 00:33:59,460
I'm looking for any water that might be in any control shaft

575
00:33:59,461 --> 00:34:01,940
or around any ceiling areas.

576
00:34:01,941 --> 00:34:04,520
And we'll actually torque on some of the controls

577
00:34:04,521 --> 00:34:07,280
to make sure that we have nothing in and around the control

578
00:34:07,281 --> 00:34:11,080
the glands themselves, checking around the port area.

579
00:34:11,081 --> 00:34:12,380
This one passed the test.

580
00:34:15,380 --> 00:34:18,660
Down here, minimal visibility makes gathering images

581
00:34:18,661 --> 00:34:19,800
even more challenging.

582
00:34:23,220 --> 00:34:25,860
Gates' camera housings can perform in waters as deep

583
00:34:25,861 --> 00:34:31,640
as 450 feet, where the pressure is more than 215 PSI,

584
00:34:32,940 --> 00:34:36,240
about 15 times greater than it is on the surface.

585
00:34:37,280 --> 00:34:42,280
Even at 100 feet, the PSI is four times greater

586
00:34:42,380 --> 00:34:47,220
than at the surface, a crushing force that also challenges

587
00:34:47,320 --> 00:34:48,620
a diver's body.

588
00:34:48,720 --> 00:34:52,020
The ocean applies pressure on all air spaces.

589
00:34:52,120 --> 00:34:55,860
Our lungs have air inside of it, our mask, our ears.

590
00:34:55,960 --> 00:35:00,440
All of these areas have to be equalized in our body.

591
00:35:00,540 --> 00:35:04,500
Otherwise, we feel squeeze and pain, and it's a bad thing.

592
00:35:07,280 --> 00:35:09,640
Even the air coursing through a diver's bloodstream

593
00:35:09,641 --> 00:35:12,180
changes with the pressure.

594
00:35:12,181 --> 00:35:14,020
This is one of diving's greatest hazards.

595
00:35:18,120 --> 00:35:20,620
Every minute a diver spends underwater,

596
00:35:20,621 --> 00:35:24,960
his blood absorbs more foreign gases, like nitrogen.

597
00:35:24,961 --> 00:35:27,600
If he ascends to the surface too quickly,

598
00:35:27,601 --> 00:35:31,080
dissolved nitrogen bubbles will turn back into gas,

599
00:35:31,081 --> 00:35:35,600
just like CO2 in a bottle of soda pop when it's uncapped.

600
00:35:35,601 --> 00:35:39,000
This potentially lethal phenomenon is called the bends.

601
00:35:42,800 --> 00:35:45,740
Divers can use a computer to calculate the amount of pressure

602
00:35:45,741 --> 00:35:50,780
they're exposed to and calibrate a slow return to the surface.

603
00:35:50,781 --> 00:35:53,420
Coming back from a deep dive may take hours

604
00:35:53,421 --> 00:35:55,520
of slow decompression.

605
00:35:55,521 --> 00:35:58,080
So every diver knows that pressure is a force

606
00:35:58,081 --> 00:35:58,920
to be reckoned with.

607
00:35:58,921 --> 00:36:05,920
The Pressure is everywhere.

608
00:36:06,020 --> 00:36:09,060
It's there when we need it.

609
00:36:09,160 --> 00:36:13,160
But when it's out of control, it can be brutally destructive.

610
00:36:13,260 --> 00:36:16,280
It can be our worst enemy underwater.

611
00:36:16,380 --> 00:36:18,200
But then we turn around and use it

612
00:36:18,300 --> 00:36:21,280
as the most powerful tool in a factory.

613
00:36:21,380 --> 00:36:24,320
Pressurized cans throughout your home,

614
00:36:24,420 --> 00:36:26,780
they not only deliver foamy lather,

615
00:36:26,781 --> 00:36:31,520
lather, they can also dispense some tasty treats.

616
00:36:31,620 --> 00:36:34,020
Whipped cream isn't the only food that's created with the help

617
00:36:34,120 --> 00:36:36,120
of pressure.

618
00:36:36,220 --> 00:36:42,020
A kernel of corn pops when the water within it turns to steam.

619
00:36:42,120 --> 00:36:46,240
Water expands 1,600 times when it becomes steam.

620
00:36:46,340 --> 00:36:49,140
In an enclosed space, that creates an incredible amount

621
00:36:49,240 --> 00:36:51,340
of pressure.

622
00:36:51,440 --> 00:36:53,880
The Industrial Revolution changed our world,

623
00:36:53,881 --> 00:36:58,880
But it could only happen after we'd harnessed steam pressure.

624
00:36:58,881 --> 00:37:01,880
So what happens if we bottle up that steam and put it to work

625
00:37:01,881 --> 00:37:04,880
in our kitchens?

626
00:37:04,881 --> 00:37:07,880
This place has almost as much in common with a science lab

627
00:37:07,881 --> 00:37:09,880
as a kitchen.

628
00:37:09,881 --> 00:37:13,880
In fact, they call it the cooking lab.

629
00:37:13,881 --> 00:37:17,880
The team here researched and photographed a multi-volume book

630
00:37:17,881 --> 00:37:22,880
called Modernist Cuisine to reveal the science behind cooking,

631
00:37:22,881 --> 00:37:25,660
and to discover new techniques.

632
00:37:29,700 --> 00:37:32,040
We use high-end technology to play with our food

633
00:37:32,041 --> 00:37:35,120
for the purpose of making it more delicious, more interesting.

634
00:37:36,480 --> 00:37:39,120
Pressure is one of any kitchen's most versatile tools.

635
00:37:40,480 --> 00:37:41,920
Many homes have pressure cookers,

636
00:37:42,360 --> 00:37:44,040
but most of us take them for granted,

637
00:37:44,760 --> 00:37:46,620
rarely stopping to think about how they work.

638
00:37:47,700 --> 00:37:49,400
When you cook in an open pot,

639
00:37:50,040 --> 00:37:51,420
liquids can never get any hotter

640
00:37:51,421 --> 00:37:54,800
than 212 degrees Fahrenheit, the boiling point of water.

641
00:37:56,360 --> 00:37:58,340
The pressure cooker is a fascinating piece of equipment

642
00:37:58,341 --> 00:38:01,900
because it allows us to cook in a high-pressure environment.

643
00:38:04,700 --> 00:38:07,500
We increase the pressure to 15 psi one bar,

644
00:38:07,860 --> 00:38:10,060
and that's in an extremely pressurized environment.

645
00:38:11,120 --> 00:38:13,780
The pressure is now twice as great as normal.

646
00:38:14,440 --> 00:38:16,760
It's equivalent to being 30 feet underwater.

647
00:38:17,900 --> 00:38:20,680
And what's strange is that the water isn't boiling.

648
00:38:21,420 --> 00:38:25,020
It'll only do that when the temperature surpasses 250 degrees,

649
00:38:25,420 --> 00:38:28,720
38 degrees higher than the boiling point at normal pressure.

650
00:38:29,080 --> 00:38:32,760
That extra heat can brown meats or caramelize vegetables faster.

651
00:38:33,560 --> 00:38:36,600
Increasing pressure can speed up cooking, create new flavors,

652
00:38:37,200 --> 00:38:41,000
and really facilitates the cooking process in general.

653
00:38:42,080 --> 00:38:44,620
Pressure cookers have been around for more than 100 years,

654
00:38:45,060 --> 00:38:47,340
but they haven't always had safety features.

655
00:38:48,640 --> 00:38:51,400
Today, the lid is held in place by heavy flanges.

656
00:38:51,420 --> 00:38:54,860
A rubber gasket forms a hermetic seal.

657
00:38:54,861 --> 00:38:58,060
And if the pot overpressurizes,

658
00:38:58,061 --> 00:39:00,860
a safety valve vents the excess pressure.

659
00:39:00,861 --> 00:39:02,260
It will never explode.

660
00:39:02,261 --> 00:39:04,260
It will just release all that steam,

661
00:39:04,261 --> 00:39:05,660
and it will be safe.

662
00:39:05,661 --> 00:39:08,180
And now it's very upset.

663
00:39:08,181 --> 00:39:11,380
Steam pressure not only cooks,

664
00:39:11,381 --> 00:39:13,380
it can also be put to work sealing food

665
00:39:13,381 --> 00:39:14,780
for long-term storage.

666
00:39:14,781 --> 00:39:17,580
So this is a pressure canner.

667
00:39:17,581 --> 00:39:19,980
Some of the features are a little bit different

668
00:39:19,981 --> 00:39:21,980
than a traditional pressure cooker.

669
00:39:23,980 --> 00:39:27,980
A pressure canner is basically a pressure cooker on steroids.

670
00:39:28,980 --> 00:39:32,980
It can withstand almost twice as many PSI as a pressure cooker.

671
00:39:32,981 --> 00:39:36,980
Rather than a rubber gasket, it has a metal-to-metal seal

672
00:39:36,981 --> 00:39:40,980
that requires heavy clamps to bolt it down and hold it in place.

673
00:39:41,980 --> 00:39:44,980
The pressure canner also works double-time.

674
00:39:44,981 --> 00:39:47,980
It sterilizes jars while sealing them.

675
00:39:49,980 --> 00:39:59,120
The process of heating and cooling the jars leaves them with a perfect vacuum seal.

676
00:39:59,121 --> 00:40:05,940
These pressure canned tomatoes can survive for centuries on the shelf.

677
00:40:05,941 --> 00:40:10,940
Another pressure cooking technique was perfected to send food into space with astronauts.

678
00:40:10,941 --> 00:40:13,820
It's the opposite of cooking under pressure.

679
00:40:13,821 --> 00:40:16,100
This is cooking in a vacuum.

680
00:40:16,101 --> 00:40:18,940
Flavor, texture, and freshness are all preserved

681
00:40:19,040 --> 00:40:22,040
as water is drawn out under less than 1,000th of a pound

682
00:40:22,140 --> 00:40:22,940
of PSI.

683
00:40:26,440 --> 00:40:29,340
This is what's called freeze drying.

684
00:40:29,440 --> 00:40:31,440
These carrots are very light.

685
00:40:31,540 --> 00:40:36,060
Only 1% to 4% of the water that was already in them

686
00:40:36,160 --> 00:40:37,060
is still there.

687
00:40:37,160 --> 00:40:40,060
So it's very, very, very dry.

688
00:40:40,160 --> 00:40:42,660
Once reconstituted, these freeze dried veggies

689
00:40:42,760 --> 00:40:45,560
look like something you'd find in a cup of dry noodle soup.

690
00:40:45,561 --> 00:40:47,360
soup or ramen.

691
00:40:47,460 --> 00:40:49,600
So with freeze drying, we're manipulating pressure

692
00:40:49,700 --> 00:40:51,700
to investigate the future of cooking.

693
00:40:54,900 --> 00:40:59,400
Lightweight dehydrated foods are perfect for outer space,

694
00:40:59,500 --> 00:41:04,220
where every ounce of launch cargo was carefully managed.

695
00:41:04,320 --> 00:41:07,220
In fact, one dish, freeze dried ice cream,

696
00:41:07,320 --> 00:41:10,520
was invented for NASA and flew on an Apollo space mission

697
00:41:10,620 --> 00:41:13,120
in the 1960s.

698
00:41:13,121 --> 00:41:15,960
And it's all thanks to the incredibly versatile force

699
00:41:16,060 --> 00:41:18,120
we call pressure.

700
00:41:18,220 --> 00:41:26,160
We use it to build and destroy, to rescue,

701
00:41:26,260 --> 00:41:31,580
to restore, and to explore.

702
00:41:31,680 --> 00:41:35,780
Wherever you go and whatever you do,

703
00:41:35,880 --> 00:41:37,880
pressure will be right there with you.

